UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211883726000869
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Nursing ethics
KW  - Southeast Asia
KW  - Nursing regulation
KW  - Licensure
KW  - Governance
AB  - Objectives
To map the intellectual structure and thematic evolution of AI ethics in nursing, to quantify Southeast Asia’s (SEA) contribution and positioning within this broader literature, and to interpret the findings across three linked domains: nursing education, clinical nursing practice, and regulatory/licensure processes.
Methods
We performed a Scopus-based bibliometric analysis of 1863 journal articles published from 2001 to 2025 (partial to 9 September 2025). The corpus was treated as a broader bibliometric literature set, within which SEA contribution was explicitly quantified using two indicators: SEA author affiliation and explicit SEA geographic context in titles, abstracts, or keywords. Descriptive indicators were generated using Bibliometrix/Biblioshiny (v5.0), and science mapping was conducted in VOSviewer (v1.6.20) using co-citation and co-word networks with predefined thresholds and normalization settings.
Results
Publication growth accelerated after 2019, with marked expansion during and after the COVID-19 period. Co-citation analysis identified five knowledge clusters: AI-ready nursing education/practice/governance; operational integration and evaluation; ethical governance for generative AI; algorithmic foundations for nursing analytics; and adoption/trust models. Co-word analysis identified four thematic domains, with strong SEA representation in applied risk-informed decision support, ethics-centered generative AI in education and care, behavioral adoption studies, and pandemic-era aged-care applications. Across clusters, SEA contributions were more visible in applied implementation themes than in concept-defining foundational literature.
Conclusions
The evidence base is expanding rapidly but remains conceptually anchored in globally developed frameworks. Findings from this broader corpus, interpreted through an explicitly quantified SEA lens, suggest three SEA-relevant policy considerations: (1) AI literacy and academic integrity safeguards in nursing education, (2) fairness checks, human oversight, and post-deployment monitoring in practice, and (3) licensure-linked guidance on AI-assisted documentation, accountability, and incident reporting.
Public Interest Summary
AI tools are increasingly used in nursing education and patient care, but rules for safe and ethical use are still developing in many Southeast Asian countries. This study analyzed published research from 2001 to 2025 to identify what is known and what is missing. We found rapid growth in studies, especially on practical uses such as risk prediction, education support, and care during COVID-19. However, much of the guiding theory still comes from outside the region. The findings suggest that nursing regulators and schools in Southeast Asia may wish to consider three recurring areas of emphasis: teaching nurses how to critically use AI, requiring safety checks and human oversight when AI is used in care, and clarifying accountability when AI supports clinical decisions or documentation. These steps can help protect patients and support responsible innovation.
